steps.instructions:
Run the starter first. It is your table from 5.3.14: three bumpers worth 1, 5 and 10, a score and a chime. Nothing on the table says what a bumper is worth.

A sprite can wear words with `.text` (5.3.15), and each bumper already keeps its own `worth`. So write that number on it. In `setup()`, where the STEP 1 comment is, add one line per bumper:

```js
b1.text = b1.worth;
b2.text = b2.worth;
b3.text = b3.worth;
```

Run it: each bumper now shows 1, 5 or 10 in its middle. The words start white. `.textColor` and `.textSize` change their colour and size, if you want to.

Find the `if (touching && !b.wasTouching) { ... }` block in `draw()`. It adds the bumper's worth and plays the chime, and it is true on one frame per hit (5.3.7). The popup goes in that same block, where the STEP 2 comment is, so each hit makes exactly one:

```js
const pop = new Sprite(b.x, b.y - 24, 28);
pop.collider = 'none';
pop.color = 'gold';
pop.text = '+' + b.worth;
pop.textColor = 'black';
pop.vel.y = -1;
```

That makes a small gold circle just above the bumper that was hit, wearing `+1`, `+5` or `+10`. `collider = 'none'` lets the ball pass through it, and moSHion switches its gravity off, so `vel.y = -1` floats it up one pixel a frame (5.3.16).

Not under `if (touching)` or `if (ball.colliding(b))` on its own: those are true on every frame of a touch, so a new popup would pile on every frame. Not in `setup()` either: that makes one popup when the game starts, and never again.
